#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152963} :activity/id #uuid "f5df7dc7-aff7-5140-9020-0f8e1d4c1cfb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050597 :topic/id #uuid "574a92ea-12bd-55d4-a248-945298d922a1" :topic/math-academy-id 2655} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152963 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153456 :tutorial/content "Given an integer $a$, the **residue class** (or **congruence class**) of $a$ modulo $n$ is the set containing all integers congruent to $a$ (modulo $n$).\n\nThe residue class of $a$ modulo $n$, denoted $[a]_n$, can be expressed using a conditional definition as follows:\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: \\mid \\: x \\equiv a \\, \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nPut another way, the residue class $[a]_n$ contains all integers that have the same remainder as $a$ when divided by $n$.\n\nFor example, the residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ contains all the even integers because they all have a remainder of zero when divided by $2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{2} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡0(mod2)} \\\\ = {2k\\end{vmatrix}k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-4,-2, 0, 2, 4…}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ contains all the odd integers because they all have a remainder of one when divided by $2$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[1]_{2} &= {x ∈ Z\\begin{vmatrix}x≡1(mod2)} \\\\ = {2k + 1\\end{vmatrix}k ∈ Z} \\\\\n&= {…,-3,-1, 1, 3, 5…}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that each element of $\\mathbb Z$ lies either in $[0]_2$ or in $[1]_2$ (but never both). Thus, these residue classes form a *partition* of the integers. We'll call this partition $\\mathbb Z_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_2 = \\big\\{[0]_2, [1]_2\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can visualize this partition using the diagram shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nSimilarly, the integers can also be partitioned into five residue classes modulo $5$.\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z_5 = \\big\\{[0]_5, [1]_5, [2]_5,[3]_5, [4]_5 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, each residue class contains all numbers that are congruent modulo $5$. We can visualize this partition of $\\mathbb Z$ using the diagram below.\n\n![](images/27/270f63afd4a0022e9f31a48e5ea5f4e799822e8c4eb5813e6c5e616e316c02e1.png)\n\nA residue class is a specific type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.** We'll learn more about different types of equivalence classes soon." :tutorial/id #uuid "26e8368d-0ec3-5b4d-8487-94376b8840d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9028 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "257362f2-5f70-5a26-bc18-e59dfebb619c" :step/math-academy-id 156895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152964}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152964 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153479 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to our diagram for visualizing the residue classes modulo $2$.\n\n![](images/f8/f84b296319e078df1827fe29dbd15fb61ce0d10cac69660f11d6a96d5c44478a.png)\n\nAny given representation of a residue class is not unique.\n\nFor example, the integers congruent to zero modulo $2$ are usually denoted $[0]_2$. In this case, $0$ is the **representative** of the class:\n\n$$\n[0]_{2} = {x \\mid x≡0(mod2)}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use *any* element of the class to represent the class. Here are some examples:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[2]_{2} &= {x\\begin{vmatrix}x≡2(mod2)} \\\\ [4]_{2}|= {x \\mid x≡4(mod2)} \\\\ [6]_{2}|= {x\\end{vmatrix}x≡6(mod2)} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use negative numbers as representatives of the class:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[- 2]_{2} &= {x\\begin{vmatrix}x≡ - 2(mod2)} \\\\ [- 4]_{2}|= {x \\mid x≡ - 4(mod2)} \\\\ [- 6]_{2}|= {x\\end{vmatrix}x≡ - 6(mod2)} \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat's important to understand is that *all of these sets are equal*:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{2} &= [2]_{2} = [4]_{2} = [6]_{2} = ⋯ \\\\\n&= [- 2]_{2} = [- 4]_{2} = [- 6]_{2} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same idea is true for $[1]_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[1]_{2} &= [3]_{2} = [5]_{2} = [7]_{2} = ⋯ \\\\\n&= [- 1]_{2} = [- 3]_{2} = [- 5]_{2} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe same idea applies to any residue class modulo $n$. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[0]_{5} &= [5]_{5} = [10]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[1]_{5} &= [6]_{5} = [11]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[2]_{5} &= [7]_{5} = [12]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[3]_{5} &= [8]_{5} = [13]_{5} = ⋯ \\\\\n[4]_{5} &= [9]_{5} = [15]_{5} = ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDespite this non-uniqueness, we usually choose the smallest nonnegative element of a residue class to represent the class." :tutorial/id #uuid "24c78443-01dd-5900-a2c6-3d43b549b1b3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9215 :tutorial/title "Representatives"} :step/id #uuid "92e60d57-6f02-556f-bc8c-58071a44c310" :step/math-academy-id 156896 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152965}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152965 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152097 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151846 :question/id #uuid "41276052-e364-593e-9557-5d645f169a74" :question/math-academy-id "e-14600"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d2f207a-8e99-5520-ab9f-266c8f54465b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152792}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to a Residue Class"} :step/id #uuid "c6e19ef8-a2b2-5b43-8bef-8b33d3e37a4d" :step/math-academy-id 156897 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152966}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152966 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152098 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151847 :question/id #uuid "33da67df-113b-58e2-ad3e-a45d1568d75d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14601"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c1fe7ea-d91e-5440-b756-37a460a18f19" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152793}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Residue Classes Containing a Given Element"} :step/id #uuid "bf05b2fd-4901-5647-b876-8df098ff47f5" :step/math-academy-id 156900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152967}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152967 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153459 :tutorial/content "Let's list some important properties of residue classes:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ *distinct* residue classes modulo $n$.\n- If $a \\equiv b \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n)$ then $[a]_n=[b]_n$. In particular, this allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n- On the other hand, two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection:\n$a \\not\\equiv b \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad [a]_n \\cap [b]_n = \\emptyset$\n- The residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers. This means the integers can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct residue classes.\n$\\mathbb{Z} = [0]_n \\cup [1]_n \\cup \\cdots \\cup [n-1]_n$\n\nThe **integers modulo** $\\boldsymbol n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{Z}_n = \\big\\{[0]_n,\\, [1]_n,\\, \\ldots,\\, [n-1]_n\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $\\mathbb Z_n$ has some interesting properties. We'll learn more about those in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "ee8e604d-6ea0-5ad4-b5c7-a21b7d6c7a8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9031 :tutorial/title "Properties of Residue Classes"} :step/id #uuid "d1e0e291-c1ef-5624-bf93-e964a6a92c9a" :step/math-academy-id 156903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152099 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151848 :question/id #uuid "edf5a7d6-354d-534b-a8d3-5b29f2357a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-14602"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6097ae19-85d5-5ca9-892d-889e843b3def" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048623}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152794}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalent Residue Classes"} :step/id #uuid "1c36a94c-bd92-5ac5-8b4d-2482b543bd82" :step/math-academy-id 156904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151850 :question/id #uuid "e8470a32-e938-5ae5-a732-688a6d62cefd" :question/math-academy-id "e-14604"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1a842b5-7256-59cd-b33f-d590bc315d84" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152789}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements"} :step/id #uuid "b2bda3d8-8738-5657-89ae-d2532c2a60cd" :step/math-academy-id 156907}] :activity/title "Residue Classes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152339}]}
